# Original appearance and lore text for the SRD 5.2.1 monsters (part 1: A to D, plus the dragons).
# Written for players: what a character could see and has probably heard. No game statistics.

LORE = {
    "Aboleth": ("A bloated, eel-like fish as long as a wagon, olive and grey, with four boneless tentacles trailing slime and three eyes stacked down its head.",
                "Sailors and cave-dwellers tell of pale things older than the gods that rule the deep lakes. They are said to remember everything, to bend minds from far away, and to keep thralls who forget their own names."),
    "Air Elemental": ("A rushing column of wind with no fixed shape, seen only by the dust, leaves and rain it drags along. A howl and a pressure on the ears announce it.",
                      "Wizards bind these spirits of open sky into service. Free ones are fickle and fast, and are said to carry the voices of the dead on high storms."),
    "Animated Armor": ("A suit of plate that walks with no one inside, the visor dark and empty, joints creaking.",
                       "Crafted by wizards and temple smiths to guard vaults and tombs, it follows one order forever and does not tire."),
    "Animated Flying Sword": ("A blade hanging in the air, point forward, humming faintly as it turns to follow movement.",
                              "An enchanted weapon set to defend a place. It hunts anyone not carrying the proper token and has no grudge beyond its command."),
    "Animated Rug of Smothering": ("An ordinary-looking carpet, patterned and fringed, that rises and ripples with a purpose of its own.",
                                   "A cruel trap left in rich halls: it lies still until a guest steps on it, then wraps around and crushes."),
    "Ankheg": ("A hulking insect the size of a horse, with a tan plated body, long feelers and heavy mandibles that tear soil like cloth.",
               "Farmers fear the patches of dead grass and collapsed tunnels that show where one has fed. It strikes from below and drags prey into the earth."),
    "Assassin": ("A quiet figure in dark, close clothing, with a hood, soft boots and a flat, patient gaze.",
                 "Hired killers are trained in poison, disguise and waiting. Cities fear them more than armies, because they strike a single person and vanish."),
    "Awakened Shrub": ("A knee-high bush that has learned to walk on its roots and rustles when it speaks.",
                       "Someone's spell has given it thought. It tends to be curious, loyal to its maker, and slow to anger."),
    "Awakened Tree": ("A broad old tree whose bark holds a faint face, with branches that move without wind and a voice like creaking boards.",
                      "A druid's magic woke it, and it now guards a grove. It remembers decades as moments and rarely hurries."),
    "Axe Beak": ("A tall flightless bird with strong legs, a thick neck and a heavy, wedge-shaped beak.",
                 "Hunters and herders keep them as mounts or fear them as pests. They are short-tempered and kick or peck anything that startles them."),
    "Azer Sentinel": ("A stocky, bronze-skinned humanoid with hair and beard that burn like torches, wearing plates of worked brass.",
                      "A craftsfolk of the Plane of Fire, honor-bound and orderly, who stand guard in forge-cities and keep oaths they have sworn."),
    "Balor": ("A towering, horned fiend with wings like rolled smoke, wreathed in flame and dragging a lashing whip and a jagged sword.",
              "Among the most feared demons, said to lead the hosts of the Abyss in war. Tales say its death throes are an explosion."),
    "Bandit": ("A rough traveler in patched leather with a blade at the hip and a hungry look.",
               "Poverty, desertion and crime drive people to the roads. They ambush the weak and avoid the well-guarded."),
    "Bandit Captain": ("A hard-eyed, scarred leader wearing the best armor stolen from the road.",
                       "Keeps a gang together with cunning, fear and a fair share of loot, and is often a former soldier."),
    "Barbed Devil": ("A lean, spined devil with bone barbs from forearm to knee, red eyes and a thin, patient smile.",
                     "A devil of the lower hells that serves as jailer and enforcer; it is said to enjoy the cries of victims it has caught."),
    "Basilisk": ("A heavy, eight-legged lizard with a rough brown hide, bony crest and eyes that glow faintly.",
                 "Legends say its gaze turns living things to stone. Statues around a lair warn of its presence."),
    "Bearded Devil": ("A wiry, scaled devil with a writhing beard of fine tendrils and a jagged glaive.",
                      "A foot soldier of the hells, ferocious and disciplined, that fights in ranks and follows commands."),
    "Behir": ("A serpentine beast like a long blue lizard with a dozen legs, curved horns and a crackling, bright maw.",
              "Mountain folk tell that it hunts giants and dragons and spits lightning. Its bones are prized by alchemists."),
    "Berserker": ("A wild fighter with matted hair, painted skin and a heavy weapon, who fights without armor.",
                  "Warriors who give themselves to rage in battle. Their clans honor them and their foes dread the sight."),
    "Black Dragon Wyrmling": ("A small dragon with dull black scales, gaunt face and acid dripping from its teeth.", ""),
    "Black Pudding": ("A heaving mass of black, glistening slime that flows along floors, walls and ceilings.",
                      "A scavenger of old dungeons that dissolves flesh and metal alike. Splitting it only makes more."),
    "Blink Dog": ("A short-furred dog with a keen, noble face and a coat of tawny and grey, that vanishes and reappears in a breath.",
                  "Fey hounds that guard against evil and play tricks on those who wrong them. Rangers say they bark in a pattern when they teleport."),
    "Bone Devil": ("A skeletal devil twice a man's height, with a tail tipped by a stinger and leathery wings.",
                   "A hellish overseer that watches over lesser devils and carries out punishments with a cold bureaucratic attitude."),
    "Bugbear Stalker": ("A broad-shouldered goblinoid with shaggy hair, long arms and a surprisingly quiet tread.",
                        "A hunter that sneaks, waits and strikes from behind. Bugbears are hired as ambushers and raiders."),
    "Bugbear Warrior": ("A large, hairy goblinoid with heavy arms and a morningstar or spear in hand.",
                        "Bugbears take what they want and bully smaller goblinoids. They love a surprise attack."),
    "Bulette": ("A land shark: a huge armored beast with a slate-grey hide, a crest of plates and jaws that crack stone.",
                "It burrows below farm fields and erupts when it feels footsteps. Folk say it is always hungry."),
    "Centaur Trooper": ("A creature with a man's torso set on a horse's body, carrying a spear or bow and moving at a gallop.",
                        "Centaurs patrol old forests and plains and respect those who respect the land. They are proud and wary of walls."),
    "Chain Devil": ("A devil wrapped in rattling chains that move on their own, with hooked links and a cruel, mocking face.",
                    "Hellish jailers that bind souls. Their chains are said to seek the guilty."),
    "Chimera": ("A roaring beast with a lion's body, a goat's head on its back and a dragon's head on a thick neck, with ragged wings.",
                "A monster of wild legend that terrorizes hills and herds, breathing fire from its dragon head."),
    "Chuul": ("A crustacean taller than a man, with a greenish carapace, stinging tentacles at its mouth and pincers like iron.",
              "Hunters of the ruined shallows. They drag prey underwater and are said to guard treasures of the deep."),
    "Clay Golem": ("A hulking, reddish figure of baked clay with a blank face and fists like boulders.",
                   "A construct built by priests and wizards, it can be hurt by little but may go berserk if its bonds slip."),
    "Cloaker": ("A broad, dark, leathery shape like a flying cloak, with a white-eyed face and a barbed tail.",
                "Lurkers of caves and cellars. They flap down on prey and drain it, and their moan chills the nerves."),
    "Cloud Giant": ("A towering, blue-skinned giant with silver or white hair, dressed in fine silks and gems.",
                    "They live in castles on the clouds. They are proud of their wealth, judge others by taste, and keep to old customs."),
    "Cockatrice": ("A small, bird-like beast with a lizard's tail, bat-like wings and a comb of red flesh on its head.",
                   "Its bite is said to stiffen the victim to stone. Farmers hate it for turning livestock into statues."),
    "Commoner": ("An ordinary villager or townsperson in plain clothes, with work-worn hands.",
                 "Farmers, cooks, carters and other folk who want to be left alone, and who flee at the first sign of danger."),
    "Couatl": ("A radiant, feathered serpent with rainbow wings, gentle eyes and a voice that carries calmly.",
               "Celestial messengers of the gods and teachers of magic. They guard sacred places and speak many languages."),
    "Swarm of Crawling Claws": ("A heap of severed hands scuttling over one another on their fingertips, grey and cold.",
                      "Left behind when a murderer is cursed or a necromancer cuts a hand free. It still wants to strangle."),
    "Cultist": ("A robed follower with a hidden symbol, carrying a knife and a murmured prayer.",
                "Ordinary folk drawn into secret worship of a terrible power, who obey the cult's leaders without question."),
    "Cultist Fanatic": ("A zealot in ritual vestments, eyes wide and voice steady, hands marked with sigils.",
                        "Devout beyond reason, they channel the favor of dark patrons and call down curses."),
    "Darkmantle": ("A ball of leathery tentacles that clings to cave ceilings, looking like a stalactite.",
                   "It drops onto prey and smothers it, and releases a cloud of inky darkness to cover its retreat."),
    "Death Dog": ("A two-headed hound with a mangy coat and glaring eyes, snarling in chorus.",
                  "Desert tales call them the mounts of the dead. Their bites are said to rot like disease."),
    "Deva": ("A tall, winged angelic being with calm features, a radiant face and a gleaming mace.",
             "Messengers who walk among mortals, often disguised, to nudge them toward good."),
    "Djinni": ("A tall, muscular being of air with bluish skin, flowing robes and a swirling lower body of smoke.",
               "Noble genies of the wind, proud and generous, who may grant wishes but bristle at being commanded."),
    "Doppelganger": ("A pale, plain-faced creature that takes the shape of any person it studies, mimicking their voice.",
                     "It replaces a victim or poses as an acquaintance to learn secrets. A bad habit of repeating questions can give it away."),
    "Dragon Turtle": ("A turtle as large as a ship, green and bronze with a spiked shell and a dragon's head and tail.",
                      "Sailors tell of islands that move and sink ships. It boils the sea with its breath."),
    "Dretch": ("A short, bloated, greasy demon with warty skin, a pig-like snout and thin limbs.",
               "The lowest ranks of the Abyss, cowardly and numerous, and used as fodder by stronger fiends."),
    "Drider": ("A drow from the waist up with a giant spider's body below, trailing silk and bristling hairs.",
               "The result of a goddess's punishment for failing a test of loyalty. Driders hate the elves who cast them out."),
    "Druid": ("A robed hermit with a wooden staff, dressed in leaves and hides, with bright, watchful eyes.",
              "Keepers of the old ways who commune with nature and punish those who despoil it."),
    "Dryad": ("A lithe fey woman with bark-like skin and hair of leaves, who steps from a tree.",
              "She is bound to her tree and her grove. She charms intruders but shields those who respect the forest."),
}
